# macOS / darwin-arm64 toolchain fix.  Without this, rules_cc's
# auto-generated `local_config_cc/BUILD` detects the system clang
# without LLD on PATH and emits `link_flags = ["-fuse-ld=ld64.lld:", ...]`
# (note the trailing colon).  Apple clang then rejects with:
#
#   clang: error: invalid linker name in argument '-fuse-ld=ld64.lld:'
#
# Putting brew's llvm bin first on the toolchain-discovery PATH makes
# rules_cc emit a clean `-fuse-ld=ld64.lld` (no trailing colon) AND
# selects the homebrew clang, which accepts the flag.
#
# `--repo_env=PATH=...` is the right knob: it scopes the override to
# repository-rule execution (where rules_cc's auto-detection runs)
# without leaking into action env, so reproducible-build hashes are
# unaffected.
#
# This is macOS-only: the `ld64.lld:` bug is an Apple-clang artefact
# and `/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin` exists only on Homebrew/Apple
# Silicon.  `--enable_platform_specific_config` makes bazel auto-apply
# the `build:macos` / `build:linux` block matching the host OS.
#
# On Linux we force the host C/C++ compiler to clang (CC/CXX), not the
# distro-default gcc.  The host-compiled runtime sources are written
# against clang: `cel_runtime.c` uses `__attribute__((musttail))` for
# the guaranteed-tail-call dispatch (GCC only gained musttail in 15;
# Ubuntu 24.04 ships gcc 13), and `cel_layout.h` relies on clang's
# handling of C11 `_Static_assert` in a C++ include context.  Using
# clang on both hosts keeps a single, consistent compiler — the wasm
# cross-compile already goes through wasi-sdk clang on every host.
# Requires `clang` on PATH (apt-get install clang); see doc/contributing.md.
build --enable_platform_specific_config
build:macos --repo_env=PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
build:linux --repo_env=CC=clang --repo_env=CXX=clang++

# Linux: link with lld, not gold.  Bazel's auto-detected Linux toolchain
# defaults to `-fuse-ld=gold`, but gold crashes linking a binary this
# large (cel-cpp + abseil + protobuf, compiled with -ffunction-sections,
# push the object's section count past what gold's section-index code
# handles): `ld.gold: internal error in set_xindex, at object.h:1050`.
# lld handles the extended section indices correctly, is far faster on
# these large static links, and is the same linker family as macOS's
# ld64.lld — keeping one LLVM toolchain on both hosts.  `-fuse-ld=lld`
# appended here wins over the toolchain's `-fuse-ld=gold` (clang honours
# the last `-fuse-ld`).  Requires `lld` on PATH (apt-get install lld).
build:linux --linkopt=-fuse-ld=lld

# ── Sanitizers: --config=asan / --config=tsan ──────────────────────
# `bazel test --config=asan //...` (likewise `--config=tsan`) builds
# every first-party target — plus cel-cpp, abseil, protobuf and
# Binaryen, which we compile from source — with instrumentation.
#
# Two things stay UNINSTRUMENTED, by construction:
#
#   - The prebuilt wasmtime archive (`@wasmtime_<host>`).  We ship it
#     as a binary, so the sanitizer sees its allocations but not its
#     source.  ASan still catches our own misuse across the boundary.
#     TSan cannot see wasmtime's internal synchronization, so a race
#     reported wholly inside wasmtime frames would be a false
#     positive — but none has appeared: `//eval:engine_test_dynamic`
#     drives 8 threads planning concurrently through wasmtime and is
#     clean, so there is deliberately no suppressions file here.  If
#     one ever becomes necessary, add it scoped to wasmtime frames
#     (`--test_env=TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...`) rather than
#     weakening this config.
#   - Exec-config build tools (protoc, //bazel:gen_runtime_catalogue).
#     Those follow `--host_copt`, which these configs deliberately
#     leave alone: instrumenting a code generator buys nothing and
#     costs build time.
#
# The wasm32-wasi cross-compile is EXCLUDED, and must be — wasi-sdk
# clang rejects the flag outright:
#
#   clang: error: unsupported option '-fsanitize=address'
#          for target 'wasm32-unknown-wasi'
#
# `wasm_cc_binary`'s outgoing transition clears the global copt /
# conlyopt / cxxopt / linkopt lists for the wasm configuration, so
# `cel_runtime.wasm` is built from toolchain + BUILD-attribute flags
# alone, whatever the host config is.  See
# third_party/wasi_sdk/wasm_cc_binary.bzl.
#
# Each config is a separate build tree: switching to or from one
# recompiles cel-cpp (~10 min from cold).  Sanitizer runs belong at
# the gate and in CI, not in the inner loop — see
# doc/implementation-plan/dev-loop-performance.md.

# Flags shared by both.  `-gline-tables-only` is exactly what the
# symbolizer needs for file:line frames, at a fraction of full `-g`'s
# object size — which matters, because CI caches a whole cel-cpp build
# per config.  `--strip=never` keeps those tables through the link.
build:sanitizer --copt=-fno-omit-frame-pointer
build:sanitizer --copt=-gline-tables-only
build:sanitizer --strip=never
# Instrumented binaries run several times slower; the stock
# 60s/300s/900s test timeouts start tripping on the e2e targets.
test:sanitizer --test_timeout=180,900,2700,5400

# absl's leak_check.cc includes <sanitizer/lsan_interface.h> — a
# toolchain header no module exports, so layering_check rejects the
# instrumented build.  The check is a header-hygiene gate, not a
# correctness gate; sanitizer runs don't need it.
build:sanitizer --features=-layering_check
# Instrumented tests carry ASan shadow + wasmtime mappings; at full
# parallelism they OOM small hosts (measured: 10 concurrent tests
# killed the 7.65 GB docker VM's processes silently mid-run; the same
# set passes at 2 jobs).  Scale test concurrency to a fraction of
# cores rather than a fixed number so big machines keep speed.
build:sanitizer --local_test_jobs=HOST_CPUS*.25

build:asan --config=sanitizer
build:asan --copt=-fsanitize=address
build:asan --linkopt=-fsanitize=address

build:tsan --config=sanitizer
build:tsan --copt=-fsanitize=thread
build:tsan --linkopt=-fsanitize=thread

# ── Cross-checkout disk cache: OPT-IN, not enabled by default ──────
# A shared --disk_cache would let a second checkout reuse compiled
# actions (measured: fresh output base → 34/34 disk-cache hits, 0
# recompiles).  It is deliberately NOT turned on here: this repo
# builds with the non-hermetic system Apple/brew toolchain, so a
# cache that outlives a compiler/SDK change could serve a stale
# object whose action key didn't capture the change.  Correctness
# first.  If you understand the trade-off, enable it per-machine in
# user.bazelrc:
#     build --disk_cache=~/.cache/celwasm-bazel-disk
# and re-baseline (bazel clean) whenever you bump Xcode / brew llvm.
# See doc/implementation-plan/dev-loop-performance.md §"cross-checkout".

# ── Local, per-developer overrides (NOT committed) ─────────────────
# Machine-specific knobs go here (disk_cache, remote_cache, --jobs).
# try-import is silent when the file is absent.
try-import %workspace%/user.bazelrc
